Given the following C `union`, what is the minimum guaranteed size of `MyUnion` in bytes on a typical 64-bit system, and why?
```c
union MyUnion {
    int i;
    float f;
    char c[8];
};
```

cards.back:
```c
#include <stdio.h>

union DataConverter {
    int i;
    float f;
};

int main() {
    union DataConverter converter;
    converter.i = 0x00000001; // Integer value 1

    printf("Integer value: %d (0x%X)\n", converter.i, converter.i);
    printf("Float representation (type punned): %f\n", converter.f);

    // To show raw bytes (endianness dependent)
    unsigned char* byte_ptr = (unsigned char*)&converter.f;
    printf("Raw bytes of float: ");
    for (int k = 0; k < sizeof(float); k++) {
        printf("%02X ", byte_ptr[k]);
    }
    printf("\n");

    return 0;
}
```
This demonstrates type punning. The output `converter.f` will likely be a very small, denormalized number (or 0.0) depending on endianness and how `0x00000001` maps to the IEEE 754 float representation.

A `union` can be initialized only with a value for its *first* declared member. For example:
```c
union MyUnion {
    int i;
    float f;
    char c;
} u = {10}; // Initializes u.i to 10
```
To initialize a different member, you must assign to it after declaration: `u.f = 3.14f;` or `u.c = 'A';`.

A "safe" variant type, often called a **tagged union**, uses a `struct` to encapsulate both a `union` and an `enum` (or similar integer type) that acts as a "tag" or "discriminator". The tag indicates which member of the `union` is currently active and valid.
```c
enum DataType { INT_TYPE, FLOAT_TYPE, STRING_TYPE };

struct Variant {
    enum DataType type; // The tag
    union {
        int i_val;
        float f_val;
        char* s_val;
    } data; // The union
};

// Example usage:
// struct Variant v;
// v.type = INT_TYPE;
// v.data.i_val = 123;
//
// if (v.type == INT_TYPE) {
//     printf("Value is an int: %d\n", v.data.i_val);
// }
```
This approach ensures that code can always check `type` before accessing `data` members, preventing undefined behavior from reading the wrong member.
